<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet  xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="calculating-the-volume-of-a-vase">
  <title>Calculating the Volume of a Vase</title>
  <objectives xml:id="objective">
    <title>Objectives</title>

    <ul>
            <li>
                        <p>
                                Estimate the volume of a (theoretical) vase by dividing the vase into small chunks and adding up their volumes. 
                    </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                        <p>
                                Consider how a limit could be used to calculate the volume exactly.
                    </p>
            </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction xml:id="problem-description">
    <title>Problem Description</title>
    <p>
      A vase has a height of 8 inches and a circular cross-section with
                    the radius <m>r</m> (in inches) given by
                    <md xml:id="eq_radius" number="yes">
        r = \frac{25}{864}h^{3}-\frac{25}{72}{h}^{2}+\frac {59}{54}h+\frac{2}{3}.
                            </md>
                            where <m>h</m> is the height (in inches) above
      the base, as shown below in <xref ref="gr_vase"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
      Our goal in this activity is to find the volume of the vase.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="gr_vase">
      <caption>A vase with circular cross section.</caption>
      <image width="50%">
              <description>
                      <p>
                              A diagram showing a vase.
                      </p>
              </description>
              <xi:include href="./vase.xml"/>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Recall that the volume for a cylinder of (constant) radius
      <m>R</m> and height <m>H</m> is given by <m>V=\pi R^2 H</m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <activity xml:id="activity">
    <title>Activity</title>
    <task xml:id="q_Max_Min_Vol">
            <introduction>
          <p>
            Let's start by getting an upper and lower bound for the
            volume of the vase:
          </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                    <introduction>
                            <p>
                            In this part we will obtain an upper limit for the volume of the vase.
                            </p>
                    </introduction>
                    <task>
                            <statement>
                      <p>
                        Where is the radius of the vase largest?
                      </p>
                            </statement>
                    </task>
                    <task>
                            <statement>
                      <p>
                        What is the radius at this point?
                      </p>
                            </statement>
                    </task>
                    <task>
                            <statement>
                      <p>
                        If the vase were a cylinder with this radius,
                        what would the volume of the cylinder be?
                      </p>
                            </statement>
                    </task>
            </task>
            <task>
                    <introduction>
                            <p>
                            In this part we will obtain a lower limit for the volume of the vase.
                            </p>
                    </introduction>
                    <task>
                            <statement>
                      <p>
                        Where is the radius of the vase smallest?
                      </p>
                            </statement>
                    </task>
                    <task>
                            <statement>
                      <p>
                        What is the radius at this point?
                      </p>
                            </statement>
                    </task>
                    <task>
                            <statement>
                      <p>
                        If the vase were a cylinder with this radius,
                        what would the volume of the cylinder be?
                      </p>
                            </statement>
                    </task>
            </task>
    </task>
        <task>
		<statement>
          <p>
            Calculate the radius of the vase at <m>h=0</m> (the base),
            <m>h=2</m>, <m>h=4</m>, and <m>h=6</m>. Use the radius at
            <m>h=0</m> as an estimate of the radius for the first two
            inches of height, the radius at <m>h=2</m> as an estimate of
            the radius for the next two inches of height, and so on. Add
            up the volumes of these pieces to get an estimate of the
            volume of the vase.
          </p>
        	</statement>
</task>
        <task>
		<statement>
          <p>
            Calculate the radius of the vase at <m>h=8</m> (the top).
            Use the radius at <m>h=2</m> as an estimate of the radius
            for the first two inches of height, the radius at <m>h=4</m>
            as an estimate of the radius for the next two inches of
            height, and so on. Add up the volumes of these pieces to get
            an estimate of the volume of the vase. How does this volume
            compare to the volumes you found in <xref ref="q_Max_Min_Vol"/>?
          </p>
        	</statement>
</task>
        <task>
		<statement>
          <p>
            Decide how can we improve on our estimate of the volume of
            the vase and implement your plan.
          </p>
        	</statement>
</task>
        <task>
		<statement>
          <p>
            How could we use a limit to calculate the volume of the vase
            exactly?
          </p>
        	</statement>
</task>
  </activity>
</worksheet>
